      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "fontSize": 28.382953631303145,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Y3jegDnt_wNByJWdgpEyA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 704.0978813674859,
      "y": 1037.817661397504,
      "width": 770.9084738017215,
      "height": 405.47076616147353,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 526339248,
      "version": 9277,
      "versionNonce": 94686947,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766240777521,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) For viewing live comments we could use something like polling. Client polls\nwith the cursor -> last commentId. Based on this we could fetch if any\ncomments have been posted or something like this.\n\n2) For the scroll through functionality, we could preheat the cache with\naround 1k entries. Now for calls the we could directly send from the cache\nitself with pagination of 50 entries.\n\n3) Modern servers can handle concurrent connections in the range of around\n100k.\n\n4) For the comments database, we'll choose DynamoDB because it is a fast,\nscalable, and highly available database. It's is a good fit for our use case\nbecause we are storing simple comments that don't require complex\nrelationships or transactions, though, other databases like Postgres or\nMySQL would work here as well.",
      "fontSize": 20.273538308073675,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) For viewing live comments we could use something like polling. Client polls with the cursor -> last commentId. Based on this we could fetch if any comments have been posted or something like this.\n\n2) For the scroll through functionality, we could preheat the cache with around 1k entries. Now for calls the we could directly send from the cache itself with pagination of 50 entries.\n\n3) Modern servers can handle concurrent connections in the range of around 100k.\n\n4) For the comments database, we'll choose DynamoDB because it is a fast, scalable, and highly available database. It's is a good fit for our use case because we are storing simple comments that don't require complex relationships or transactions, though, other databases like Postgres or MySQL would work here as well.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
