    {
      "id": "HCq5hZtkx_UA7OvUQuhuO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 899.2456707790341,
      "y": -70.27292141765497,
      "width": 985.4800415039062,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1988709987,
      "version": 138,
      "versionNonce": 1182770019,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766246752525,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For this breakdown we are going to design request level rate limiting for social media platform's API ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For this breakdown we are going to design request level rate limiting for social media platform's API ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1539.8405433698238,
      "y": 71.16284025961522,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 1325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 595,
      "versionNonce": 995843629,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766240163110,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Latency\n- Good solution: Websocket\nChallenges: Websocket is bidirectional but poses us some challenges.\nMaintaining the connection and non-compatibility with certain firewalls could\npose challenge. Also ws is good for the cases where read to write ratio is\neven like chat application. For this particular case the number of people\nreading comments vs mentioning is pretty high hence, picking SSE for this\nscenario is good.\n\n- Great solution: SSE\nSame challenges with the proxies and load balancer. Comment comes, gets\npersisted in the db from there for the given videoId all the clients receive\nthe given comment. For the normal posting comment we use a simple http\nrest API.\n\n2) Scalability of Handling Millions of Live Streams with Thousands of\nViewers. \n- For SSE the server needs to maintain an open connection for each viewer.\n\n- BOE calc: (1M active live streams * 1k) this will be the total connections\nrequired. Per server we can have upto 100k.\n\n- Bad Solution: We scale horizontally with load balancer and pub sub.\nThe first thing we do is there is a huge difference in the read write ratio\nof comments so, to scale accordingly we separate this out. Upon connection\nmessage is sent to the server for key(videoId) : List(sse connections)\nupdates. For new comments, the server goes through the list of\nconnections for the given video and send the message. How does our\nrealtime messaging server knows there is a new comment => we use EDA\npubsub.\n\n- Good Solution: For the current implementation all the servers are informed\nwhen a new comment is received which is highly inefficient. Instead we\ncreate partition in the pub sub based on the various videos. Now the\nservers can subscribe to only those channels whose viewers connections are\nthere with the server.\n\n- Great Solution: We can further improve this by distribution of viewers of\na given video to same servers. We can store a dynamic mapping of a videoid\nto given servers. And then the load balancer could make sure to distribute\nload to the given servers for that particular service. With this we can use\nzookeeper.\n\nGreat Solution: Using a Dispatcher Service\nThe Dispatcher Service inverts the pub/sub model by directly routing each\nnew comment to the specific Realtime Messaging Server responsible for that\nliveVideoId, eliminating broadcast complexity and scaling issues with read-\nheavy workloads. It maintains dynamic mappings (stored in memory and\nsynced with Zookeeper/etcd) of server-to-videoId associations, updated\nvia heartbeats and registration protocols as servers join, scale, or fail.\nMultiple Dispatcher instances run behind a load balancer, consulting shared\ncoordination data to enable consistent direct routing and provide\nredundancy against failures.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Latency\n- Good solution: Websocket\nChallenges: Websocket is bidirectional but poses us some challenges. Maintaining the connection and non-compatibility with certain firewalls could pose challenge. Also ws is good for the cases where read to write ratio is even like chat application. For this particular case the number of people reading comments vs mentioning is pretty high hence, picking SSE for this scenario is good.\n\n- Great solution: SSE\nSame challenges with the proxies and load balancer. Comment comes, gets persisted in the db from there for the given videoId all the clients receive the given comment. For the normal posting comment we use a simple http rest API.\n\n2) Scalability of Handling Millions of Live Streams with Thousands of Viewers. \n- For SSE the server needs to maintain an open connection for each viewer.\n\n- BOE calc: (1M active live streams * 1k) this will be the total connections required. Per server we can have upto 100k.\n\n- Bad Solution: We scale horizontally with load balancer and pub sub.\nThe first thing we do is there is a huge difference in the read write ratio of comments so, to scale accordingly we separate this out. Upon connection message is sent to the server for key(videoId) : List(sse connections) updates. For new comments, the server goes through the list of connections for the given video and send the message. How does our realtime messaging server knows there is a new comment => we use EDA pubsub.\n\n- Good Solution: For the current implementation all the servers are informed when a new comment is received which is highly inefficient. Instead we create partition in the pub sub based on the various videos. Now the servers can subscribe to only those channels whose viewers connections are there with the server.\n\n- Great Solution: We can further improve this by distribution of viewers of a given video to same servers. We can store a dynamic mapping of a videoid to given servers. And then the load balancer could make sure to distribute load to the given servers for that particular service. With this we can use zookeeper.\n\nGreat Solution: Using a Dispatcher Service\nThe Dispatcher Service inverts the pub/sub model by directly routing each new comment to the specific Realtime Messaging Server responsible for that liveVideoId, eliminating broadcast complexity and scaling issues with read-heavy workloads. It maintains dynamic mappings (stored in memory and synced with Zookeeper/etcd) of server-to-videoId associations, updated via heartbeats and registration protocols as servers join, scale, or fail. Multiple Dispatcher instances run behind a load balancer, consulting shared coordination data to enable consistent direct routing and provide redundancy against failures.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
